            "Every test rests on the five Cs: <strong>Consent</strong> (informed, voluntary, opt-out rather than opt-in — a patient may decline without losing care; mandatory testing is unlawful except in the narrow circumstances the HIV and AIDS Act allows, such as screened blood donation), <strong>Confidentiality</strong>, <strong>Counselling</strong> before and after, <strong>Correct results</strong> by the national algorithm, and <strong>Connection to care</strong> the same day. <strong>Provider-initiated testing and counselling</strong> is offered to every inpatient, every antenatal client at the first visit (and again in the third trimester or labour if negative), every person with tuberculosis, a sexually transmitted infection, malnutrition or an unexplained illness, and the partners and children of anyone who tests positive (index testing); <strong>self-test kits</strong> are sold and distributed in Ghana and a reactive self-test is always confirmed. The national algorithm is <strong>serial rapid testing</strong> — a first screening test, a different confirmatory test if reactive, and a third tie-breaker if they disagree — with a retest to verify the diagnosis before antiretroviral therapy begins. Results are given <strong>in person, in private, by the person who counselled, never by phone, text, a relative or across a ward</strong>; a positive result is followed by time for the reaction, an immediate explanation that treatment works and life expectancy is near normal, a plan for partner notification and testing with the client's agreement, screening for tuberculosis and other illness, and a walk — not a referral slip — to the ART clinic. A negative result is a moment for prevention: the window period, retesting in six weeks after a recent exposure, condoms, PrEP for those at ongoing risk.",
