            "Without intervention, 15–45% of babies born to women with HIV are infected — during pregnancy, in labour and through breastfeeding; with full prevention the risk falls below 2%, and Ghana's remaining transmission is a map of the gaps: women who book late or not at all, who test negative early and acquire HIV later in pregnancy, who are not retained on treatment, or whose babies are lost to follow-up. The programme is <strong>Option B+</strong>: <strong>every pregnant woman is tested at the first antenatal visit and again in the third trimester or labour if negative</strong>; a positive woman <strong>starts lifelong TLD the same day</strong> (dolutegravir is the preferred drug in pregnancy), has a <strong>viral load at 34–36 weeks</strong>, and can deliver vaginally if suppressed, with no fetal scalp electrodes, routine episiotomy or prolonged rupture of membranes, and the cord not milked. The baby receives <strong>nevirapine syrup daily for six weeks</strong> (twelve weeks with zidovudine added if the mother's viral load is high or unknown), <strong>BCG and the full immunisation schedule</strong>, <strong>co-trimoxazole from six weeks</strong>, and the <strong>DNA PCR at six weeks</strong>, nine months and the final test at eighteen months or three months after weaning. Feeding: <strong>exclusive breastfeeding for six months</strong>, then breastfeeding with complementary foods to at least twelve months, while the mother takes her ART and stays suppressed — <strong>mixed feeding before six months is the most dangerous choice</strong> because it inflames the infant gut; replacement feeding is advised only where it is affordable, feasible, acceptable, sustainable and safe, which for most Ghanaian families it is not. The mother's partner is offered testing, her own follow-up continues after delivery when women are most often lost, and the baby's clinic card records exposure discreetly.",
