            "Ghana's <strong>HIV and AIDS Act, 2016 (Act 938)</strong> makes it unlawful to discriminate against a person because of HIV status in employment, education, housing or health care, prohibits testing without consent except where the law specifies, and protects confidentiality — <strong>a person's status may be disclosed only with their consent</strong>, to those directly involved in their care who need to know, or in the narrow circumstances the Act allows. On the ward this means <strong>no ‘HIV’ written on bed boards, folder covers or ward lists, no colour codes, no separate cups, cutlery or toilets, no double-gloving for a blood pressure, no telling the relatives or the employer, no discussing the patient in the corridor</strong>, and no nurse refusing to care for or perform a procedure on a patient because of status — which is professional misconduct. Stigma, not the virus, is what keeps people from testing, from collecting tablets in their home town, from telling a partner and from breastfeeding safely; the nurse's ordinary, unflinching, bare-handed kindness in front of the family is the most effective anti-stigma intervention there is. <strong>Disclosure</strong> to a partner is encouraged and supported — assisted disclosure in the clinic, with safety assessed first where violence is possible — and a patient who refuses to tell a partner at risk is counselled repeatedly; in extremis the Act allows a clinician to inform the partner after documented counselling. <strong>Adolescents</strong> who grew up with HIV need their own clinics, transition planning, honest talk about sex and contraception, school support and peers. Living well is the goal and it is realistic: ART taken daily, a viral load checked yearly, TB screened at every visit, cervical screening, vaccination (people with HIV receive the routine vaccines; live vaccines such as yellow fever are given when the CD4 is above 200), <strong>sleeping under a treated net</strong> because malaria is more severe, boiled or treated water, well-cooked food, no raw eggs, extra energy and protein, no smoking, limited alcohol, exercise, treatment of depression, and family planning — pregnancy is safe and the babies can be born free of HIV.",
