            "<strong>Innate immunity</strong> is present from birth and the same for every invader: intact skin and mucous membranes, stomach acid, cilia and cough, tears and saliva, the normal flora, <strong>neutrophils and macrophages</strong> that engulf organisms, natural killer cells, complement proteins that punch holes in bacteria, and the inflammatory response and fever. It is fast and has no memory. <strong>Adaptive immunity</strong> is learned and specific: <strong>B lymphocytes</strong> mature into plasma cells that make antibodies, and <strong>T lymphocytes</strong> — CD4 helper cells that coordinate everything, CD8 cytotoxic cells that kill infected and malignant cells, and regulatory cells that switch the response off — and both leave <strong>memory cells</strong> so that the second meeting with an organism is faster and stronger, which is the whole basis of vaccination. The antibodies have jobs: <strong>IgM</strong> is the first made in an acute infection and the ABO antibody; <strong>IgG</strong> is the memory antibody, the most abundant, and <strong>the only one that crosses the placenta</strong> — which is why a newborn is protected for the first months by the mother's immunity, why anti-D harms the fetus, and why an infant's positive HIV antibody test proves only exposure; <strong>IgA</strong> guards the mucosal surfaces and is the antibody of breast milk; <strong>IgE</strong> binds mast cells and drives allergy and the response to worms. Immunity is <strong>active</strong> when the body makes it itself (after infection, or a vaccine) — slow to develop, long-lasting — and <strong>passive</strong> when antibody is given ready-made (across the placenta, in breast milk, or as immunoglobulin, antitoxin or antivenom) — immediate, and gone within weeks to months.",
